description: At a join (a loop head, an if's merge) the IR gives the block a param for every local in scope, read or not, and no pass removes the unread ones. Each unread param is still an arg on every edge into the block, so liveness keeps its value, and the previous generation's, alive to the end of the scope.
 
 In xsofy's terrain/place-lake! (2,802 block params), 1,451 (51%) are never read; they are only passed on to the next join. Pruned, the values live into one block fall from 137 to 21.
 
 On the llvm target every non-tail call saves its live values in a continuation record, so this is code and work: pruning those params in the llvm lowering cut the world dump's saved fields from 72,865 to 43,508 (the largest record from 160 to 30), the module from 55 to 42 MB, and assemble by 7%. On WasmGC they are locals, so the cost is smaller there but not zero.
 
 Proposed, in let-go's IR (~/development/let-go, its ir.build or a pass): a dead-block-param pass (a param is needed if an instruction or terminator reads it, or an edge passes it into a needed param; to a fixpoint), or build pruned SSA in ir.build. The llvm lowering's own pruning can then go.
